Id: pass025w
Severity: WARN
Category: accounts
Check: check_passwdspec
Fix: Lock the account (usermod -L, and usermod -s /usr/sbin/nologin) or remove it (userdel); if it is still wanted, have its password changed (chage -d 0 forces that at the next login).
References: shadow(5); chage(1); usermod(8); CIS Ubuntu Linux 24.04 LTS Benchmark v2.0.0, 5.4.1.5 (Ensure inactive password lock is configured) and 5.4.1.6 (Ensure all users last password change date is in the past); CIS Controls v8, 5.3 (Disable Dormant Accounts)
Controls: CIS 5.3; NIST AC-2(3), IA-5(1); ISO 5.17, 5.18; CE User access control

The listed login ID's password has expired, or its account expiry date
has passed, yet the password is not locked and the account keeps a
login shell. In /etc/shadow the password's age is the date of its last
change plus the maximum age (what chage -l shows as "Password
expires"); the account's own end is the expiry date (chage -E). Past
either, login through PAM demands a new password or refuses the
account, but nobody disabled it: the hash is still valid wherever
expiry is not enforced (a service that checks the hash itself, sshd
with UsePAM no and a key in authorized_keys), and a chage -E -1 or a
changed date brings the account straight back. What it usually means
is an account whose owner has left or stopped using it, which the
benchmark asks be locked or removed. The finding says how long ago it
expired.

Not reported: an account past its inactivity period (last change plus
maximum plus the inactive days of chage -I), which the system has
locked itself, as CIS 5.4.1.5 asks; a last change of 0, which forces a
new password at the next login on purpose; and accounts whose password
is locked (! or * in front of the hash) or whose shell is nologin or
false, which cannot log in whatever the dates say. Today's date comes
from date(1), so an offline root is judged against the auditing host's
clock.
